ivers who have to  quit their jobs that help take care of  them let's talk about that because her  Carol's husband Mike becomes her  caregiver absolutely and this runs for  ten years now which is which is  extraordinary what what is the toll on  Mike over this period of time mentally  and physically it takes a huge toll on  him I mean he's so lovely he's a looking  sickness and this is what I signed up  for yeah and you see him combing her  hair and putting makeup on her and  making the beds and he said look just  the roles are reverse you used to do  this and I signed up for this he said  I'm not gonna put her in a nursing home  you know I can take care of it but he  couldn't take care of it and over the  years his blood pressure went up his  weight went up you know he became  anxious he had panic attacks and it got  to the point where he just simply  couldn't do it and I think I think  that's a lesson for this is a big  message you may think you're tough you  know he's a Staten Island cop regardin  york city cop tough as nails until  alzheimer's you cannot do it alone and  that's sort of a message that everybody  has to take away from this I think it is  and you know he had some help first of  all dr. Gayatri Devi is his neurologist  image she has been unbelievable you know  your your physician but then you sort of  cross over into friend also she's been  both of that to both of them  I find that same friction yeah I have  the physician hat I have the journalist  hat I have the person hat and you know  over ten years you become friendly with  these I was struck by that number ten  years that's a long time for an  Alzheimer's patient isn't it it is and  the Alzheimer's Association told us they  don't know of another report where  somebody was followed with Alzheimer's  and certainly in their caregiver for ten  years I know the average time that  between diagnosis and dying is about  four to eight years it can go as long as  as 20 years  there's a spectrum as dr. Devi just  wrote the Newland Journal are we just  diagnosing Alzheimer's earlier now is  that part of this or this just happens  to be a unique I think in her case it's  very unusual that she lived as long I  was just talking to dr. Devi this  morning she said she was surprised that  she lived this longer but you know there  there is hope I mean there are new  approaches to diagnosis and one of the  big things is that we think that we're  we're treating people probably way too  late that's like giving somebody lipitor  after their fifth heart attack and  they're already in heart failure you got  a it turns out that the the problems  inside the brain the pathological  changes start 10 20 30 years before the  symptoms even start and the other thing  I want to point out is people have to  have discussions when they're still with  it about what their desires are if I get  to the point where or I have to be in a  in a nursing home is that ok you know if  you can't handle me alone if I become  demented or otherwise infirmed  then is it okay to bring in outside help  I mean that is so important there are  tough discussions and Americans don't  like to have those discussions at that  end of life we have to do it dr. Jon  LaPook thank you very much and you can  see all of John's report and more  tomorrow night on 60 minutes right here  on CBS
 

